# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: alfe-plugin-model — the alfe plug-in system (epic); EPURE and EPUREE are the first two plug-ins
#+STATUS: NEXT
#+PRIORITY: P2
#+TYPE: epic
#+DEPENDS: -
#+BRANCH: master (pjb's standing delegation: direct commits, no MR)

See also: =alfe.plan= (Phase 5), =issues/closed/alfe-cli.issue= (the
parsed-but-inert =--epure= flag), =issues/closed/alfe-backend-bricscad.issue=
§7 and =issues/closed/alfe-backend-autocad.issue= §4 (the EPURE bullets
that were deferred to this ticket), =issues/closed/transmit-options.issue=
(=*AUTOLISP-PLUGIN-OPTIONS*=, whose plug-in activation function was "To
Be Defined").

* Context

State on 2026-09-21, verified in the tree (not from memory):

- =--epure= / =$AUTOLISP_EPURE= is parsed into =cli-options-epure-p=
  (=source/cli.lisp=, =clautolisp/autolisp-cli/source/options.lisp=),
  echoed as =*AUTOLISP-PLUGIN-OPTIONS*= = =(EPURE)=, and surfaces in
  =--help= as "Enable the EPURE plugin hook". *No backend reads the
  slot*: it does nothing. The manual and the spec's EPURE section
  describe the behaviour of the legacy bash wrapper
  (=outils-autolisp/autolisp-script/autolisp-deprecated=) as if alfe had it.
- The only EPURE-related code in alfe runs the other way:
  =discover-bricscad-profile= + =build-launch-argv= launch BricsCAD with a
  /clean/ profile so the default profile does not auto-load EPURE/SCHMS+
  and stall the =/b= script.
- Every EPURE-style customisation so far was a special case in the
  wrapper; the first sentence of the spec's EPURE section already says
  "this option is specific to a single user. A way of defining such
  options as modular plug-ins should be found".

The original ticket (2026-05-24) listed three open design decisions
(discovery, lifecycle hooks, per-backend gating). They are settled below.

* Decisions

** Taken by pjb, 2026-09-21

1. alfe gets a *plug-in system*. The first plug-in is *EPURE*, the second
   *EPUREE* (=--epuree=).
2. A plug-in can *define CLI options, with arguments*.
3. A plug-in can *intervene, modify or complete* alfe's behaviour *at
   every stage* of the process.
4. Plug-ins are *installed* under
   =${PREFIX}/share/clautolisp/plug-in/${name}/${name}.lisp= (source) or
   =${name}.${alfe-version}-${impl}.plugin= (compiled), or *locally in the
   right XDG directory*. They are written in *Common Lisp*.
5. EPURE follows what the legacy tools did
   (=~/works/sncf-reseau/scripts/{autocad,bricscad}-epure= and the old
   =autolisp= wrapper): the plug-in defines =--epure=; when alfe launches
   AutoCAD or BricsCAD the launch options are modified as
   =scripts/${cad}-epure= does; because EPURE is started by *running a
   script*, the script alfe generates calls it at the right moment (or
   replaces it). =--mode batch= may not be usable, in which case COM
   (automation) starts the EPURE script and the run-time. If the run-time
   is what is started, the GUI stays blocked for the duration — a problem
   or not depending on the use.
6. EPUREE (=--epuree=) is simpler: it loads =epuree= (a future ALPM package,
   =~/works/sncf-reseau/src/epuree/=) at the right moment.

** Design choices taken here (to be reviewed; each is one line to change)

The normative text is the spec chapter "Plug-ins"
(=documentation/alfe--specifications.org=). Summary:

- *Load all, activate by flag.* Every installed plug-in is loaded at
  start-up so its options exist for the parser; only an *active* plug-in
  (its activation flag, =--plugin NAME=, or =$ALFE_PLUGINS=) has its hooks
  called. A plug-in option without its plug-in active is a usage error.
- *Namespaced options.* A plug-in named =NAME= may only define long
  options =--NAME= or =--NAME-…=; no short options. Collisions are
  registration errors, never silent shadowing.
- *One hook mechanism, three kinds* (event / filter / collect), one
  catalogue of named hook points covering parse → backend selection →
  plan → workdir → run-common.lsp → launcher script → launch argv →
  engine started → each action → shutdown → exit code → error.
- *Zero plug-ins active = zero behaviour change.* Every hook call site is
  a no-op that returns its input unchanged when no active plug-in
  registered that hook.
- *A plug-in that fails to load is a warning*, never fatal: =--help=,
  =--version= and every plug-in-free run keep working.
- *Compiled plug-ins are named for the alfe version and the CL
  implementation* because a fasl is valid for exactly one of each; the
  compiled file is preferred when it is at least as new as the source.

* Decomposition

| Issue                        | Deliverable                                                        | Depends on                  |
|------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------|
| alfe-plugin-spec-and-manual  | Design in the spec + user manual + man page + release notes        | -                           |
| alfe-plugin-loader           | Discovery, search path, source/compiled loading, =--plugin*= options, install layout | spec-and-manual |
| alfe-plugin-options          | Plug-in-defined CLI options with arguments, activation, help, =cli-options= slots | loader        |
| alfe-plugin-hooks            | =alfe.plugin= API, hook catalogue, instrumentation of cli/backends | options                     |
| alfe-plugin-epure            | EPURE plug-in (BricsCAD + AutoCAD launch under EPURE)              | hooks                       |
| alfe-plugin-epuree           | EPUREE plug-in + =epuree.alpm=                                     | hooks                       |
| alfe-plugin-epure-windows-validation | Real-CAD validation of EPURE on the Windows runner         | epure, =windows-access=     |

* Acceptance criteria (epic)

- All sub-issues closed (the last one, the Windows validation, may stay
  open as its own issue: it needs hardware this session does not have).
- =alfe --epure --bricscad …= and =alfe --epure --autocad …= produce the
  launch command, launcher script and run-common.lsp the legacy wrapper
  produced (asserted with the mock CAD, =--print-command= and
  =*host-os-override*=).
- =alfe --epuree …= puts the ALPM load of =epuree= in front of the user's
  actions, on every backend.
- The old =cli-options-epure-p= slot and the built-in =--epure= spec are
  gone (no silent legacy alias: the option now comes from the plug-in).
- =make -C autolisp-front-end test-sbcl test-ccl check-spec-coverage
  check-user-visible-documentation= green; spec, user manual, man page,
  =--help= and =RELEASE_NOTES.org= in step.

* Migration note

When each sub-issue closes, update the "Deferred items" notes of
=issues/closed/alfe-backend-bricscad.issue= and
=issues/closed/alfe-backend-autocad.issue= to name the closing commit.
